# Differential cases (ADR-0020) of #66 "S2a.16 Comparison and sort order": where jq 1.8.2's jv_cmp stops at its
# comparison depth (ADR-0029, ADR-0037, GLOSSARY "Comparison depth"). jvp_cmp raises "Comparison too deep" when a pair
# lies deeper than 10 000 (MAX_CMP_DEPTH, src/jv_aux.c), the check coming first on every call: an array's elements are
# one level below it; an object's key list is one level below it and each key two, and its values one. So N arrays
# around null compare at N = 10 000 and not at 10 001, and an object at depth 9 998 compares while one at 9 999 does
# not, even when it has no value deeper (its keys are at 10 001); an empty object at 10 000 does not either (its key
# list is at 10 001). The deep values are documents of nested arrays, read at jq's parse depth (10 000 arrays is the
# most, F-22), wrapped once more by the filter. == and != are #65's Equality (jv_equal), whose same-value fast path
# gives true on [.] == [.] at 10 001 levels (the #66 ticket body). Written by a generator in the scratchpad, not by
# hand. Format: oracle/src/main/java/io/github/subapavel/jqj/oracle/diff/DiffCase.java. Expected outputs live in
# compare-depth.rec, written by scripts/oracle-record.sh only. Ids are immutable.

